	"identifying_description": "Carved from a single piece of wood, grain oriented vertically, with shallow relief decoration. The upper half of the figure has smoothly finished wood, with tool marks becoming evident on the lower half. There is a pinhole at the top of the head which may once have had an added element. The figure has two necklaces of tube-shaped beads (formed from thin copper-alloy sheet) strung on 2-ply cords (one red, one white) and a third necklace made from solid copper alloy thick-gauge wire. It also has a waist bracelet of copper alloy beads on a cord made of three 2-ply strands Z-twist fibers. These beads are made from strips of copper-alloy. White pigment has been applied to the hair and scarification marks. The rest of the figure appears to have a surface of black pigment and possiblyoil.",
	"web_text": "This possibly unique female figure in the corpus of Luba sculpture is distinguished by the fine detail of its elegant elongated form. It embodies concepts of beauty, memory, power, and authority in Luba culture, where women served important roles as spirit mediums, emissaries, and advisors to Luba kings and chiefs through the twentieth century. Thin arms loop toward the chest with hands placed on the breasts in a gesture expressing a belief that only a woman’s body is strong enough to contain powerful spirits (such as the spirit of a chief or king) and “precepts of royalty.\". The facial features of the figure are delicately rendered. A large coiffure extends down the neck, adorned with copper-alloy beads, and a string of copper-alloy beads accentuates the hips. When the figure was created, copper was scarce and highly valued. The elongated torso draws attention to a scarification pattern of keloids (raised scars) that surrounds and emphasizes the umbilical hernia, the point of maternal contact. Beads and scarification are mnemonic devices. For example, each bead is a morpheme—a unit of form and meaning purposefully placed or arranged. Luba scarification patterns are named and have specific meanings; a woman decides what patterns she will wear and how she will be seen by others. These attachments and inscribed elements allow a knowing viewer or handler of the figure to “read” the sculpture. Women are acknowledged as the primary keepers of the history and memories of Luba peoples. This figure, which expresses Luba ideals of beauty, also presents the female body as a locus for Luba memory. Fine Arts Museums of San Francisco, de Young 125 (California: Cameron + Company, 2021), p. 245, entry by Christina Hellmich.",
